ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529041 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529041 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528812 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529041 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529041 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529041 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186528515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529041 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c008af03-a6bf-532f-9d1b-deae201a2d39" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529068 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529068 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529068 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529068 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529068 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529068 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529068 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529068 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186528558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529068 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e1f86725-a9a0-5a34-a3e3-7d5e69393d36" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529080 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528664 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529080 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529080 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529080 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529080 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529080 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529080 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529080 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186528664 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529080 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f148f577-f7bb-5169-8125-f796d9a90542" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529081 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528405 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529081 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529081 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529081 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529081 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529081 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529081 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529081 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186528748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529081 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f62f58ca-a3ef-528b-83c7-bf2ccd3c2d04" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529088 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529088 #edb/ref 1069 "q-50239" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529088 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529088 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/96/96f465c4da3f2e482fa0224e23564d27e363a727602ec05ed6daad8213807211.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=5$ and $x=\\sqrt{3},$ where $x\\ge 0,$ as shown above. Which of the following gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529088 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0b6da66a-d01e-54fd-b6f1-7c1c4b4f3048" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529088 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing our first function in terms of $y,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=5\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{5-2{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & =\\sqrt{5-2{y}^{2}} \\\\ 3 & =5-2{y}^{2} \\\\ 2{y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $y=-1$ to $y=1.$ \n\nLet's draw our horizontal arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/54/54fbb219933d282480f70d8f87cbb8225c126979c58a5be6faed0a98eaf97c51.png)\n\n \n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $x=\\sqrt{3},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $x=\\sqrt{5-2{y}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}({[\\sqrt{5-2{y}^{2}}]}^{2}-{[\\sqrt{3}]}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}([5-2{y}^{2}]-3)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529089 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529089 #edb/ref 1069 "q-123369" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529089 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529089 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/fb/fb3afbc1bebf1abc77b0495135e02b26da362ffb346aa261d42ba43dca94ec66.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by $x={e}^{{y}^{2}}$ and $x={e}^{y+2},$ as shown above. Which of the following gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated ${360}^{∘}$ about the $y$-axis?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529089 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0bef34f9-b7a4-5afd-919c-8a8bc54bd07d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529089 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $f(y)={e}^{{y}^{2}}$ and $g(y)={e}^{y+2}.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ {e}^{{y}^{2}} & ={e}^{y+2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =y+2 \\\\ {y}^{2}-y-2 & =0 \\\\ (y+1)(y-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $y=-1$ and $y=2.$ \n\nLet's draw our horizontal arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/b6/b6db4894c0cf0204ae676a0350771cf47359031f30fb975d48d5864b1592e673.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $x=f(y)={e}^{{y}^{2}},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $x=g(y)={e}^{y+2}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}({[{e}^{y+2}]}^{2}-{[{e}^{{y}^{2}}]}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}({e}^{2y+4}-{e}^{2{y}^{2}})\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529090 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529081 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529090 #edb/ref 1069 "q-123488" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529090 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529090 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b2/b2888d7fef98dacad56792a627ef1554ed28def615f1431de35d3ba9820fe0de.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by $x={y}^{2}+2$ and $y=2-x,$ as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529090 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "10f20a34-5dca-5f03-b661-36330ae0c9f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529090 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing our second function in terms of $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=2-x\\,⟹\\,x=2-y.\n$$\n\nLet $f(y)={y}^{2}+2$ and $g(y)=2-y.$\n\nNow, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ {y}^{2}+2 & =2-y \\\\ {y}^{2}+y & =0 \\\\ y(y+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $y=-1$ to $y=0.$    \n\nLet's draw our horizontal arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/af/afe7bb6fbc5f523e04bdd8c5bc9e899af356729da0e4c7822bd7619dfeb3cb95.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $x=f(y)={y}^{2}+2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $x=g(y)=2-y.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}({[2-y]}^{2}-{[{y}^{2}+2]}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}(4-4y+{y}^{2}-({y}^{4}+4{y}^{2}+4))\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}(-{y}^{4}-3{y}^{2}-4y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(-\\frac{{y}^{5}}{5}-{y}^{3}-2{y}^{2})|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =\\pi [0-(\\frac{1}{5}+1-2)] \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529095 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529095 #edb/ref 1069 "q-122546" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529095 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529095 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/8c/8ce8a8061ec087c39ce93d73d0566832f33c4e6ce5c2f5185170c9372a0d88fa.png)\n\nConsider the finite region above bounded by $y=2x+2$ and $y=\\sqrt{4x+4},$ as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x\\text{-axis.}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529095 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "17abd4d5-764f-50c1-82c3-4fcec4b8a574" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529095 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $f(x)=2x+2$ and $g(x)=\\sqrt{4x+4}.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ 2x+2 & =\\sqrt{4x+4} \\\\ 2(x+1) & =\\sqrt{4x+4} \\\\ 4(x+1)^{2} & =4x+4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+1 & =x+1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+x & =0 \\\\ x(x+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $x=-1$ to $x=0.$\n\nLet's draw our vertical arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/0a/0a6bdcc071500ac8dd40143c3219a14c80fc73d543d7307cb2c880a22db793a3.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $y=f(x)=2x+2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $y=g(x)=\\sqrt{4x+4}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}({[\\sqrt{4x+4}]}^{2}-{[2x+2]}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}[4x+4-(2(x+1))^{2}]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}[4(x+1)-4({x}^{2}+2x+1)]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}[x+1-({x}^{2}+2x+1)]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}(-{x}^{2}-x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {(-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =4\\pi (0-(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =4\\pi (\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529114 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529114 #edb/ref 1069 "q-122558" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529114 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529114 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b9/b9f1ea82756eed4a815afb54defd8425631a57a0bcd1dfaba2dbf5b24b43b518.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by $x=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ and $x=3\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,$ as shown above. Which of the following gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated ${360}^{∘}$ about the $y$-axis?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529114 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3d1bfa1c-c252-5832-a4ec-b38acfcd7492" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529114 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $f(y)=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ and $g(y)=3\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\ y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =0 \\\\ (y-3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $y=1$ and $y=3.$ \n\nLet's draw our horizontal arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/54/54278318429e01bc3fa2c0325fea7c7a918aacf096b1510d8acc87d3ffc53a73.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $x=f(y)=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $x=g(y)=3\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}({[3\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]}^{2}-{[y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}(9(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)^{2}-{y}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}(9-{y}^{2})(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529117 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529117 #edb/ref 1069 "q-11107" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529117 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529117 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/0f/0f94662dcae88371bb47d9748d002e38c6e521b0ba689e31e782ce288d266a58.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=2$ and ${x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}=1,$ where $x\\ge 0,$ as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529117 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4870784d-cfa2-5882-86d4-3e6f72784ac7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529117 #edb/ref 1074 "Given that $x\\ge 0,$ writing our functions in terms of $y,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=2\\, & ⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{2+{y}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}=1\\, & ⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{1+2{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2+{y}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+2{y}^{2}} \\\\ 2+{y}^{2} & =1+2{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ y & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $y=-1$ to $y=1.$    \n\nLet's draw our horizontal arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/41/4177abe03f95230a3176738cddbc556d2e4c3703fe72c1391a53c1ecb5f82181.png)\n\n  \n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $x=\\sqrt{1+2{y}^{2}},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $x=\\sqrt{2+{y}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}({[\\sqrt{2+{y}^{2}}]}^{2}-{[\\sqrt{1+2{y}^{2}}]}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}((2+{y}^{2})-(1+2{y}^{2}))\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3})|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi [(1-\\frac{1}{3})-((-1)+\\frac{1}{3})] \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529132 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529132 #edb/ref 1069 "q-362200" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529132 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529132 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded by $y=x+6$ and $y={x}^{2}.$ Find the volume $V$ of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529132 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "61a0b761-b748-5011-958d-51f405f814ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529132 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $f(x)=x+6$ and $g(x)={x}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ x+6 & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-x-6 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)(x+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $x=-2$ to $x=3.$\n\nLet's draw our vertical arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/dd/dd8dc332f56d3b851647e5ed0fa8208eb5d3daf6e704a42ab5cffdb78ed78585.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $y=g(x)={x}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $y=f(x)=x+6.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{3}((x+6)^{2}-({x}^{2})^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{3}({x}^{2}+12x+36-{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+6{x}^{2}+36x-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5})\\,{|}_{-2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{27}{3}+54+108-\\frac{243}{5})-(-\\frac{8}{3}+24-72+\\frac{32}{5})] \\\\  & =\\frac{500\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529150 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529150 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86118" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529150 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529150 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded by $y=2-x$ and $y=4-{x}^{2}.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529150 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "89b9341d-d36f-5db4-a196-956e022f6dd9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529150 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $f(x)=2-x$ and $g(x)=4-{x}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ 2-x & =4-{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-x-2 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $x=-1$ to $x=2.$\n\nLet's draw our vertical arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/ce/ce74cc02284807691e00fc59d00240520d392cdc01b8efafcd66518d717a607e.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $y=g(x)=2-x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $y=f(x)=4-{x}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}({[4-{x}^{2}]}^{2}-{[2-x]}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}({x}^{4}-9{x}^{2}+4x+12)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529152 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529152 #edb/ref 1069 "q-122004" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529152 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529152 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/9c/9c5d2e2426a817b62f8cd834d7d31786c5f2dfd52a7b784fd0bf337abed6c4fc.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by $y={e}^{2-x}$ and $y={e}^{{x}^{2}},$ as shown above. Which of the following gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529152 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8f211adc-046d-5c01-ba2b-26f9e0e2be96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529152 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $f(x)={e}^{2-x}$ and $g(x)={e}^{{x}^{2}}.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ {e}^{2-x} & ={e}^{{x}^{2}} \\\\ 2-x & ={x}^{2} \\\\ 0 & ={x}^{2}+x-2 \\\\ (x+2)(x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $x=-2$ to $x=1.$\n\nLet's draw our vertical arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/8a/8a7f49165698bdac791a31a058c3228e6e2beab9f5ee9393971aa0e9aecd27b8.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $y=g(x)={e}^{{x}^{2}},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $y=f(x)={e}^{2-x}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1}({[{e}^{2-x}]}^{2}-{[{e}^{{x}^{2}}]}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1}({e}^{4-2x}-{e}^{2{x}^{2}})\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529156 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529156 #edb/ref 1069 "q-11094" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529156 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529156 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/2b/2bf88537e9a37404dd10dd99769f6933879d07c1ffbb913adc2a4f52b11f48d6.png)\n\nConsider the finite region above bounded by $y=\\sqrt{x}$ and $y={x}^{3},$ as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529156 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a5df2e25-639b-5613-82c1-85dbfc89566d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529156 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ and $g(x)={x}^{3}.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & ={x}^{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{x}-{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{x}(1-{x}^{5/2}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $x=0$ and $x=1.$ \n\nLet's draw our vertical arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/0c/0cdcf559f1498045d3d8cd725593da6adb0459addb1df7cc3c4167b2fd9b6214.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $y=g(x)={x}^{3},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $y=f(x)=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[\\sqrt{x}]}^{2}-{[{x}^{3}]}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-{x}^{6})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{7}}{7})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{7})-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529158 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529158 #edb/ref 1069 "q-50227" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529158 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529158 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/37/37bf4ff4ad6eb63c9bf51bc5fd188ddc0f5bd58bfd4a097617f56fb5de151300.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by $y={x}^{3}$ and $y=x$ where $x\\ge 0,$ as shown above. Which of the following gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529158 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "abb34aa3-eece-5767-8c0d-162486a46e00" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529158 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $f(x)={x}^{3}$ and $g(x)=x.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ {x}^{3} & =x \\\\ {x}^{3}-x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence $x=0$ and $x=\\pm 1.$ But, since $x\\ge 0,$ the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1.$\n\nLet's draw our vertical arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/f8/f8850008be348bd78f48837def8691d3a4155e70d13909369c70da2eb31fffb4.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $y=f(x)={x}^{3},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $y=g(x)=x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[x]}^{2}-{[{x}^{3}]}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{x}^{6})\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529165 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529165 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86145" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529165 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529165 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded by $x={y}^{2}-6$ and $x=-2.$ Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529165 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bb5bb053-af9b-561d-bd9f-9db0ee360eaf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529165 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $f(y)={y}^{2}-6$ and $g(y)=-2.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ {y}^{2}-6 & =-2 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ y & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $y=-2$ and $y=2.$\n\nLet's draw our horizontal arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/ba/ba9886ace429eee313ac208f7a8aebd93993c734e4382656dcf31ef15d04cd00.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $x=g(y)=-2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $x=f(y)={y}^{2}-6.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}({[{y}^{2}-6]}^{2}-{[-2]}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}({y}^{4}-12{y}^{2}+32)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{{y}^{5}}{5}-4{y}^{3}+32y)|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{32}{5}-32+64)-(-\\frac{32}{5}+32-64)] \\\\  & =\\frac{384\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529176 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529176 #edb/ref 1069 "q-123365" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529176 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529176 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/55/55308256c72aaf122c04c0b7f3a987fccd72d2513dd4edb6cf0ea4ce857522e5.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by $y=2x+2$ and $x=\\sqrt{y-2},$ as shown above. Which of the following gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529176 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d84428ea-0ba4-5227-96d6-43b34422e545" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529176 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing our first function in terms of $y,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=2x+2\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}y-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $f(y)=\\frac{1}{2}y-1$ and $g(y)=\\sqrt{y-2}.$\n\nNow, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}y-1 & =\\sqrt{y-2} \\\\ y-2 & =2\\sqrt{y-2} \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =4(y-2) \\\\ (y-2)^{2}-4(y-2) & =0 \\\\ (y-2)((y-2)-4) & =0 \\\\ (y-2)(y-6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $y=2$ to $y=6.$\n\nLet's draw our horizontal arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/8e/8e621715ded3c5ed527d2367785707939b1e3593707eaafe0b4c5e4509883e49.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $x=f(y)=\\frac{1}{2}y-1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $x=g(y)=\\sqrt{y-2}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{6}({[\\sqrt{y-2}]}^{2}-{[\\frac{1}{2}y-1]}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{6}(y-2-{[\\frac{1}{2}y-1]}^{2})\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529181 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529181 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86111" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529181 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529181 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded by $y=8x$ and $y={x}^{4}.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529181 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "de3cc4c9-f0a7-5d21-89e4-4f5e7276aeec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529181 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $f(x)=8x$ and $g(x)={x}^{4}.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ 8x & ={x}^{4} \\\\ {x}^{4}-8x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{3}-8) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=2.$\n\nLet's draw our vertical arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/ac/ac3248f7ada22b91c42971a0a85692625321cafa82ced12215671435ab584d00.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $y=g(x)={x}^{4},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $y=f(x)=8x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({[8x]}^{2}-{[{x}^{4}]}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(64{x}^{2}-{x}^{8})\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529193 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529193 #edb/ref 1069 "q-50225" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529193 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529193 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/9b/9b328ecd6017922e429f11c57aca0ff452e4dc6a1476b14a448c6c9edd6b6679.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by $x=2-\\sqrt{y}$ and $y=1-{x}^{2}$  from $y=0$ to $y=1,$ as shown above. Which of the following gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated ${360}^{∘}$ about the $y$-axis?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529193 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f666ceb4-bd3b-5b00-9ee5-dd4ca3fe5f6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529193 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing our second function in terms of $y,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=1-{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{1-y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $f(y)=2-\\sqrt{y}$ and $g(y)=\\sqrt{1-y}.$\n\nLet's draw our horizontal arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/7d/7d92b9f69ab27a2620ae94bfb4937509fc9db6dafdec4384af5de33a4dfecd30.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $x=g(y)=\\sqrt{1-y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $x=f(y)=2-\\sqrt{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[2-\\sqrt{y}]}^{2}-{[\\sqrt{1-y}]}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[2-\\sqrt{y}]}^{2}-{[\\sqrt{1-y}]}^{2})\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
